    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Uterus larger than dates: resolved (O26.841)</content>. Explained by a twin pregnancy. The dates are confirmed: the crown-rump lengths agree with her last period within three days, well inside the seven days that would call for redating before 14 weeks. No fibroids or molar features. [S]<list><item>Resolved: the diagnosis is recorded as a dichorionic diamniotic twin pregnancy (PRB-004).</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Twin pregnancy, dichorionic diamniotic (O30.041)</content>. Chorionicity is settled now, before 14 weeks, when the twin-peak sign is most reliable. A dichorionic pregnancy carries no risk of twin-to-twin transfusion, but twins raise the risks of preterm birth, preeclampsia, growth problems, gestational diabetes, anemia, and postpartum hemorrhage. Intake results: blood group A, RhD positive, antibody screen negative; rubella immune; hepatitis B surface antigen, HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis C negative; urine culture no growth. [S]<list><item>Aneuploidy screening: nuchal translucency 1.6 and 1.8 mm today; cell-free DNA drawn today, as she chose; chorionic villus sampling and amniocentesis remain available.</item><item>Aspirin 81 mg at bedtime from 12 weeks, next week, until delivery.</item><item>Detailed anatomy scans at 19 weeks, then growth scans every four weeks.</item><item>Visits every two to four weeks.</item><item>Weight gain of about 17 to 25 kg; an extra 300 to 600 calories a day with protein and iron-rich foods.</item><item>Teaching on the signs of preterm labor; no bed rest or activity restriction is needed for twins alone.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nausea and vomiting of pregnancy (O21.9)</content>. Improving on doxylamine with pyridoxine; no weight loss. [S]<list><item>Continue at bedtime; stop when the nausea settles.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Iron deficiency anemia in pregnancy (O99.011)</content>. Twins increase iron needs; she is taking alternate-day iron without side effects. [S]<list><item>Continue ferrous sulfate every other day.</item><item>Blood count and ferritin at 19 weeks; intravenous iron if they do not improve.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure and urine protein</td><td>Every prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>106/64 mmHg; urine protein negative</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fetal hearts and fundal height</td><td>Every prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two live fetuses on the scan; uterus about 14 weeks in size</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight gain (about 17 to 25 kg for twins from a normal BMI)</td><td>Every prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>58.2 kg, up 0.2 kg since intake</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Dating and chorionicity scan</td><td>Before 14 weeks in every twin pregnancy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dichorionic diamniotic; crown-rump lengths 45 and 46 mm; dates confirmed</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Nuchal translucency for each fetus</td><td>At 11 to 14 weeks, as she chooses</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1.6 mm (twin A) and 1.8 mm (twin B)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Cell-free DNA screening</td><td>From 10 weeks, as she chooses</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Drawn today; in twins it cannot say which twin is affected and fails more often</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>First prenatal tests (blood group, antibody screen, rubella, hepatitis B, HIV, syphilis, hepatitis C, urine culture)</td><td>At the first prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed today: RhD positive; all normal or negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin and ferritin</td><td>At intake, at the anatomy-scan visit, at 24 to 28 weeks, and in the third trimester</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL and ferritin 14 ng/mL at intake; next at 19 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Low-dose aspirin</td><td>Daily from 12 weeks until delivery; review at every visit</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Prescribed today to start at 12 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Maternal-fetal medicine consultation</td><td>Early in a twin pregnancy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; surveillance plan agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Detailed anatomy scans</td><td>At 18 to 22 weeks</td><td>Due</td><td>Booked for 19 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Perinatal mood screen (EPDS)</td><td>Each trimester</td><td>Up to date</td><td>6 at intake, item 10 scored 0; next in the second trimester</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Preterm labor warning signs</td><td>Every prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Signs of preterm labor taught; no activity restriction</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>The cause of the large uterus and the dating of the pregnancy established at the 11-week scan. Outcome by 2026-03-17: Dichorionic diamniotic twins; dating by her last period confirmed by the crown-rump lengths [S]</item><item>An informed choice about aneuploidy screening by 14 weeks. Outcome by 2026-03-17: Chose nuchal translucency for each twin with cell-free DNA; declined diagnostic testing for now [S]</item><item>Hemoglobin and ferritin in the normal range for pregnancy by the third trimester. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item><item>Growth discordance under 20 percent at every growth scan. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item><item>A written delivery plan agreed by 34 weeks. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Multifetal Gestations: Twin, Triplet, and Higher-Order Multifetal Pregnancies: ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 231 (2021); Prediction and Prevention of Spontaneous Preterm Birth: ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 234 (2021); Committee Opinion No 700: Methods for Estimating the Due Date (2017); Screening for Fetal Chromosomal Abnormalities: ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 226 (2020); Anemia in Pregnancy: ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 233 (2021); Aspirin Use to Prevent Preeclampsia and Related Morbidity and Mortality: Preventive Medication (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Prenatal visit with the anatomy scans at 19 weeks; sooner for bleeding, pain, or vomiting that stops her keeping fluids down. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-12: prenatal visit at 19 weeks with the detailed anatomy scans and cervical length. [S]</paragraph></text>

